logitech rolled out a new headset the g4  33 and it sets itself apart from  companies other headsets by sticking to  the basics it's not flashy  it offers decent sound and it's the most  comfortable headset from Logitech thus  far but it is gonna cost you a hundred  dollars alright let's break down the g4  33 an immediate stand out from handling  this headset is that it feels solid it's  not flimsy or fragile which is  surprising giving it's plastic frame it  also doesn't make annoying creaking  sounds like other headsets built with  plastic since its intended to be the  sort of everyday life style of headset  the gaudy gamer aesthetic is gone the g4  33 comes with plenty in the package  you'll get two 3.5 millimeter cables one  that's braided and approximately six and  a half feet long and another that's a  little shorter just under 5 feet more  suitable for mobile use the flexible  microphone detaches with ease and makes  sense for mobile scenarios it comes with  an audio and mic splitter in case you're  using a device that doesn't have the  combo 3.5 millimeter port a USB digital  to analog converter or DAC allows the g4  33 to use DTS headphone X which is  virtual 7.1 surround sound but it's  exclusive to pcs you also get a little  mesh travel pouch to take them on the go  in addition to the stock mesh ear pads  you have the choice of microfiber at  your pads to swap in please pop out  easily but it takes a little bit of work  to get back in I much prefer to the  microfiber since the mesh feels little  rough and doesn't grip my head as well  now I haven't exactly been a fan of  logitech's headsets in the past since I  found them to be uncomfortable for long  sessions however the g4 33 is a  significant improvement although it's  not the best in terms of comfort I can  use these through an entire workday or  gaming session without irritation it  tends to clamp a bit tightly but not to  the point where it's unbearable for my  own personal setup I switch between  Sennheiser HD 5 5/8 and audio Technica's  8 th M 50 X's depending on the situation  now you won't get the same exact level  of sonic fidelity with a g4 33 but the  sound impressive within the gaming  headset space I listen to a wide range  of music genres and use lossless files  to get the cleanest audio possible  strong bass shines through even tapping  into a little bit of the sub bass for  real deep rattling notes without  drowning out other frequencies mids and  highs come out clean but they tend to  sound distorted up louder volumes come  on when it comes to gaming the G 433  sounds just as good if not better than  headsets in this price range battlefield  one felt full and immersive with  explosions going off gunfire to my left  and right planes flying overhead and  soldiers screaming commands across the  trenches even out louder volumes all of  these different sounds didn't seem harsh  or overbearing Forza horizon 3 sounded  great as well although he didn't need  detailed sound for tactical purposes it  was nice to hear the engines roar over  its diverse soundtrack  this is what you should expect from a  gaming headset that costs $100 through  the USB DAC and logitech gaming software  you can customize your EQ settings and  activate DTS headphone X which is a  solution for virtual 7.1 surround sound  although it gave battlefields a little  bit more depth than a better soundstage  Audio felt a little hollow as if the  events occurred in the concert hall  rather than that of the battlefield  microphone quality is a definite  highlight the micro pop filter helps  suppress the harshness of s and P sounds  and chatting in discord was clean the  boom mic does a decent job of not  picking up outside noises while it  wouldn't exactly be in audio files first  choice the G 433 succeeds as a  jack-of-all-trades so if you're willing  to spend a hundred dollars on a gaming  headset this should be on your radar if  you're looking to complete your gaming  setup check out our comparison of razors  and hyper X's mice and subscribe to  GameSpot for more thanks for watching
 

